13-36. An improvised sling may be made from any available non-stretching piece of cloth, such as a utility uniform shirt or trousers, poncho, or shelter half. Slings may also be improvised using the tail of a coat (figure 13-4), belt (figure 13-5), or a piece of cloth may also be used for support. A sling is used to place the supporting pressure on the casualty’s uninjured side (figure 13-6). The hand on the supported arm is positioned slightly higher than the elbow, showing the fingers.

{{img:0}}

13-37. The sling is secured by bringing the ends of the sling up and tie them at the side (or hollow) of the neck on the uninjured side (figure 13-7). The end or corner of the sling is then twisted and tucked at the elbow (figure 13-8).

{{img:1}}

13-38. Apply swathes to the injured arm by wrapping the swathe over the injured arm, around the casualty’s back, and under the arm on the uninjured side. Tie the ends on the uninjured side (figure 13-9 on page 128).

You may use any large piece of cloth, Service member’s belt, or pistol belt, to improvise a swathe. A swathe is applied to an injured leg by wrapping the swathe around both legs and securing it on the uninjured side.

{{img:2}}

**WARNING:** The swathe should not be placed directly on top of the injury but positioned either above or below the fracture site.

13-39. Seek medical assistance. Notify medical personnel, watch closely for development of life-threatening conditions and, or impaired circulation to the injured extremity.

13-41. To splint an upper extremity fracture—

- Have the casualty sit up.

- Have someone support the fractured extremity.

Remove all jewelry from the fractured extremity.

Expose the fracture site.

