16-16. Non-venomous snakes include snakes that lack venom glands or fangs and pose no direct threat to humans. Non-venomous snakes play an essential role in controlling rodent populations and maintaining ecological balance. Examples of non-venomous snakes are boas, pythons, corn, and garter snakes.

16-17. There are approximately 130 different varieties of nonvenomous snakes in the U.S. They have oval-shaped heads and round eyes. Unlike venomous snakes, discussed below, nonvenomous snakes do not have fangs with which to inject venom. Figure 16-1 depicts the characteristics of a nonvenomous snake.

